  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\n`save_etag()` (line 267–273) calls `fileno()`, `fstat()`, `ftruncate(fd, 0)` whenever the underlying fd is a regular file. It does not check `OutStruct.regular_file`, which is FALSE for stdout even when shell-redirected to a file. When `--etag-save -` is used with append redirection (`>>`), an HTTP 2xx/3xx response containing an ETag header causes `ftruncate` to destroy all pre-existing content in the append target before writing the ETag value. The peer controls the trigger via the response status code and ETag header.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```sh\npython3 poc_etag_truncate.py <sha>\n```\n\n1. Write `PRESERVE\\n` to a log file\n2. Run `curl --etag-save - http://server/ >> logfile` where server returns `ETag: \"remote\"`\n3. Log file now contains only `\"remote\"\\n` — `PRESERVE` is gone\n\n**Control:** Same setup but server returns no ETag header → `PRESERVE` retained.\n\n## Root cause\n\n```c\n// src/tool_cb_hdr.c:267-273\nint fd = fileno(etag_save->stream);\nif((fd != -1) &&\n   !curlx_fstat(fd, &file) &&\n   (S_ISREG(file.st_mode) &&    // ← only checks physical file type\n    toolx_ftruncate(fd, 0)))     // ← truncates regardless of ownership\n```\n\n`S_ISREG` alone cannot distinguish a curl-owned ETag output file from stdout redirected to a regular file. \n\n## Remediation\n\nGuard `ftruncate` with `etag_save->regular_file` (the semantic stream flag) in addition to `S_ISREG`.\n\n## Impact\n\nA remote server can destroy arbitrary pre-existing content in any regular file the user's shell has opened for append on stdout. The attacker controls the trigger (any 2xx/3xx response with an ETag header) and the replacement content (the ETag value). The user must have chosen `--etag-save -` and append redirection, but the target file, its prior contents, and the decision to append are entirely the user's — curl silently violates the append contract. In scripting contexts where multiple curl invocations append ETags to a shared log, a single malicious server response wipes the entire log history.v",
